il Pipelines businesses consist of large-scale natural gas and crude oil pipelines,\nL107@P2: respectively, primarily situated in Canada and the U.S. TCPL is also the general partner of TC PipeLines, LP\nL108@P2: (PipeLines LP), a limited partnership that owns interests in U.S. natural gas pipelines.\nL109@P2: Natural Gas Pipelines\nL110@P2: TCPL’s natural gas pipeline systems consist of a network of more than 60,000 kilometres (km) (37,000 miles) of wholly\nL111@P2: owned and operated natural gas pipelines, and more than 8,800 km (5,500 miles) of partially owned natural gas\nL112@P2: pipelines. The network connects major natural gas supply basins and markets, transporting approximately 20 per cent of\nL113@P2: the natural gas consumed in North America or 14 billion cubic feet (Bcf) of natural gas per day, which is delivered to\nL114@P2: local distribution companies, power generation facilities and other businesses in markets across North America. The\nL115@P2: Company’s U.S. Natural Gas Pipelines also include regulated natural gas storage facilities in Michigan with a total\nL116@P2: capacity of 250 Bcf.\nL679@P15: PipeLines LP, which occurred as a result of the public issuance of common units by PipeLines LP in November 2009.\nL680@P15: Comparable EBIT in 2008 was $2,030 million excluding the $279 million of gains received by Portland and GTN from\nL681@P15: the bankruptcy distributions with Calpine and the $17 million of proceeds received by GTN from a lawsuit settlement\nL682@P15: with a software supplier.\nL683@P15: Wholly Owned Canadian Natural Gas Pipelines Net Income\nL684@P15: Year ended December 31 (millions of dollars) 2010 2009 2008\nL685@P15: Canadian Mainline 267 273 278\nL686@P15: Alberta System 198 168 145\nL687@P15: Foothills 27 23 24\nL688@P15: NATURAL GAS PIPELINES – FINANCIAL ANALYSIS\nL689@P15: Canadian Mainline The Canadian Mainline is regulated by the NEB under the National Energy Board Act (Canada).\nL690@P15: The NEB sets tolls that provide 