, Energy commits a significant portion of its supply to sales contracts that\nL2029@P45: are medium-term to long-term while retaining an amount of unsold supply in case of unexpected plant outages and in\nL2030@P45: order to provide operational flexibility in managing the Company’s portfolio of wholly owned assets. This unsold supply\nL2031@P45: is subsequently sold under shorter-term forward arrangements or into the spot market and is exposed to fluctuating\nL2032@P45: power and natural gas market prices. Additionally, as power sales contracts expire, new forward contracts are entered\nL2033@P45: into at the prevailing market prices.\nL2034@P45: Bruce B volumes are subject to a floor price mechanism. When the spot market price is above the floor price, Bruce B’s\nL2035@P45: non-contracted volumes are subject to spot price volatility. When spot prices are below the floor price, Bruce B receives\nL2036@P45: the floor price for all of its output. However, Bruce B’s results during this period remain subject to the impact of\nL2037@P45: fluctuating spot prices upon the settlement of fixed-price contract sales. The majority of contracted sales at Bruce B\nL2038@P45: expired at December 31, 2010. All Bruce A output is sold into the Ontario wholesale power spot market under a fixed\u0002price contract with the OPA and 100 per cent of Eastern Power sales volumes are sold under long-term contracts. As\nL2615@P59: based on historical correlations and volatilities in commodity prices, interest rates and foreign exchange rates, and\nL2616@P59: assumes that future price movements will follow a statistical distribution. Although losses are not expected to exceed\nL2617@P59: the statistically estimated VaR on 95 per cent of occasions, losses on the other five per cent of occasions could be\nL2618@P59: substantially greater than the estimated VaR.\nL2619@P59: TCPL’s estimation of VaR includes wholly owned subsidiaries and incorporates relevant risks associated with each market\nL2620@P59: or business unit. The calculation does not include the regulated natural gas pipelines, as the nature of the\nL2621@P59: rate-regulated pipeline business reduces the impact of market risks. TCPL’s Board of Directors has established a VaR\nL2622@P59: limit, which is monitored on an ongoing basis as part of the Company’s risk management policy. TCPL’s consolidated\nL2623@P59: VaR was $12 million at December 31, 2010 (2009 – $12 million).\nL2624@P59: Counterparty Credit Risk\nL2625@P59: Counterparty credit risk represents the financial loss the Company would experience if a counterparty to a financial\nL2626@P59: instrument failed to meet its obligations in accordance with the terms and conditions of its contracts with\nL2627@P59: the Company.\nL2628@P59: Counterparty credit risk is managed through established credit management techniques, including conducting financial\nL2629@P59: and other assessments to establish and monitor a counterparty’s creditworthiness, setting exposure limits, monitoring\nL2630@P59: exposures against these limits, using master netting arrangements and obtaining financial assurances where warranted.\nL2631@P59: In general, financial assurances include guarantees, letters of credit and cash. The Company monitors and manages its\nL2632@P59: concentration of counterparty credit risk on an ongoing basis. The Company believes these measures minimize its\nL2975@P70: December 2009 and did not identify any material issues. The HS&E management system is subject to ongoing internal\nL2976@P70: review to ensure that it remains effective as circumstances change.\nL2977@P70: As one of TCPL’s priorities, safety is an integral part of the way its employees work. In 2010, one of the Company’s\nL2978@P70: objectives was to sustain health and safety performance. Overall, the Company’s safety frequency rates in 2010\nL2979@P70: continued to be better than most industry benchmarks.\nL2980@P70: The safety and integrity of the Company’s existing and newly-developed infrastructure also continued to be top\nL2981@P70: priorities. All new assets are designed, constructed and commissioned with full consideration given to safety and\nL2982@P70: integrity, and are brought into service only after all necessary requirements have been satisfied. The Company expects to\nL2983@P70: spend approximately $250 million in 2011 for pipeline integrity on its wholly owned pipelines, an increase of\nL2984@P70: approximately $95 million over 2010 primarily due to increased levels of in-line pipeline inspection on all systems and\nL2985@P70: pipeline enhancements in areas of population encroachment. Under the approved regulatory models in Canada,\nL2986@P70: non-capital pipeline integrity expenditures on NEB-regulated pipelines are treated on a flow-through basis and, as a\nL2987@P70: result, these expenditures have no impact on TCPL’s earnings. Under the Keystone contracts, pipeline integrity\nL2988@P70: expenditures are recovered through the tolling mechanism and, as a result, these expenditures have no impact on\nL2989@P70: TCPL’s earnings. Expenditures for GTN may also be recovered through a cost-recovery mechanism in its rates if threshold\nL2990@P70: expenditures are achieved. TCPL’s pipeline safety record in 2010 continued to be above industry benchmarks. TCPL\nL2991@P70: experienced no pipeline breaks in 2010. Spending associated with public safety on the Energy assets is focused primarily\nL3978@P94: Other comprehensive loss (245) (160) (99)\nL3979@P94: Balance at end of year (877) (632) (472)\nL3980@P94: 3,381 3,499 3,317\nL3981@P94: Total Shareholders’ Equity 15,747 14,872 12,963\nL3982@P94-95: The accompanying notes to the consolidated financial statements are an integral part of these statements.NOTES TO CONSOLIDATED FINANCIAL STATEMENTS 97\nL3983@P95: TRANSCANADA PIPELINES LIMITED\nL3984@P95: NOTES TO CONSOLIDATED FINANCIAL STATEMENTS\nL3985@P95: NOTE 1 DESCRIPTION OF TRANSCANADA PIPELINES LIMITED’S BUSINESS\nL3986@P95: TransCanada Pipelines Limited (TCPL or the Company) is a wholly owned subsidiary of TransCanada Corporation (TransCanada) and is a\nL3987@P95: leading North American energy company. TCPL operates in three business segments, Natural Gas Pipelines, Oil Pipelines and Energy, each of\nL3988@P95: which offers different products and services.\nL3989@P95: Natural Gas Pipelines\nL3990@P95: The Natural Gas Pipelines segment consists of the Company’s investments in regulated natural gas pipelines and regulated natural gas storage\nL3991@P95: facilities. Through its Natural Gas Pipelines segment, TCPL owns and operates:\nL3992@P95: • a natural gas transmission system extending from the Alberta/Saskatchewan border east into Quebec (Canadian Mainline); ´\nL3993@P95: • a natural gas transmission system in Alberta and northeastern British Columbia (B.C.) (Alberta System);\nL3994@P95: • a natural gas transmission system extending from producing fields primarily located in Texas, Oklahoma, the Gulf of Mexico and Louisiana to\nL3995@P95: markets primarily located in Wisconsin, Michigan, Illinois, Ohio and Indiana, and to regulated natural gas storage facilities in\nL3996@P95: Michigan (ANR);\nL3997@P95: • a natural gas transmission system extending from the B.C./Idaho border to the Oregon/California border (GTN);\nL3998@P95: • a natural gas transmission system extending from central Alberta to the B.C./Idaho border and to the Saskatchewan/Montana border\nL3999@P95: (Foothills);\nL4000@P95: • a natural gas transmission system extending from the Powder River Basin in Wyoming to Northern Border in North Dakota (Bison);\nL4001@P95: • natural gas transmission systems in Alberta that supply natural gas to the oil sands region of northern Alberta and to a petrochemical\nL4002@P95: complex at Joffre, Alberta (Ventures LP); and\nL4003@P95: • a natural gas transmission system in Mexico extending from Naranjos, Veracruz to Tamazunchale, San Luis Potosi (Tamazunchale).\nL4021@P95: TCPL does not operate but has ownership interests in natural gas pipelines and natural gas marketing activities as follows:\nL4022@P95: • a 44.5 per cent interest in a natural gas transmission system that connects with the Canadian Mainline near Waddington, New York, and\nL4023@P95: delivers natural gas to customers in the northeastern U.S. (Iroquois);\nL4024@P95: • a 46.5 per cent interest in a natural gas transmission system extending from Mariquita to Cali in Colombia (TransGas); and\nL4025@P95: • a 30 per cent interest in a natural gas transmission system extending from Loma de la Lata, Argentina to Concepcion, Chile (Gas Pacifico), ´\nL4026@P95: and in an industrial natural gas marketing company based in Concepcion (INNERGY). ´\nL4027@P95: TCPL is constructing and expects to operate a natural gas pipeline in Mexico that will transport natural gas from Manzanillo to Guadalajara\nL4028@P95-96: (Guadalajara).98 NOTES TO CONSOLIDATED FINANCIAL STATEMENTS\nL4029@P96: Oil Pipelines\nL4030@P96: The Oil Pipelines segment consists of a wholly owned and operated crude oil pipeline extending from Hardisty, Alberta to U.S. markets at\nL4031@P96: Wood River and Patoka in Illinois (Wood River/Patoka) and from Steele City, Nebraska to Cushing, Oklahoma (Cushing Extension). The\nL4032@P96: Company plans to expand and extend the oil pipeline to the U.S. Gulf Coast (U.S. Gulf Coast Expansion) (collectively, Keystone) with physical\nL4033@P96: construction to commence upon receipt of final permits.\nL4034@P96: Energy\nL4035@P96: The Energy segment primarily consists of the Company’s investments in electrical power generation plants and non-regulated natural gas\nL4036@P96: storage facilities. Through its Energy segment, the Company owns and operates:\nL4037@P96: • natural gas-fired cogeneration plants in Alberta at Carseland, Redwater, Bear Creek and MacKay River;\nL4038@P96: • a waste-heat fuelled power plant at the Cancarb thermal carbon black facility in Medicine Hat, Alberta (Cancarb);\nL4039@P96: • a natural gas-and oil-fired generating facility in Queens, New York, consisting of multiple units employing steam turbine, combined-cycle\nL4040@P96: and combustion turbine technology (Ravenswood);\nL4041@P96: • a natural gas-fired, combined-cycle power plant in Halton Hills, Ontario (Halton Hills);\nL4042@P96: • hydroelectric generation assets located in New Hampshire, Vermont and Massachusetts (TC Hydro);\nL4043@P96: • a natural gas-fired, combined-cycle plant in Burrillville, Rhode Island (Ocean State Power);\nL5131@P120: There is currently no uniform industry methodology for estimating VaR. The use of VaR has limitations because it is based on historical\nL5132@P120: correlations and volatilities in commodity prices, interest rates and foreign exchange rates, and assumes that future price movements will\nL5133@P120: follow a statistical distribution. Although losses are not expected to exceed the statistically estimated VaR on 95 per cent of occasions, losses\nL5134@P120: on the other five per cent of occasions could be substantially greater than the estimated VaR.\nL5135@P120: TCPL’s estimation of VaR includes wholly owned subsidiaries and incorporates relevant risks associated with each market or business unit. The\nL5136@P120: calculation does not include the regulated natural gas pipelines, as the nature of the rate-regulated pipeline business reduces the impact of\nL5137@P120: market risks. TCPL’s Board of Directors has established a VaR limit, which is monitored on an ongoing basis as part of the Company’s risk\nL5138@P120-121: management policy. TCPL’s consolidated VaR was $12 million at December 31, 2010 (2009 – $12 million).NOTES TO CONSOLIDATED FINANCIAL STATEMENTS 123\nL5139@P121: Counterparty Credit Risk\nL5140@P121: Counterparty credit risk represents the financial loss the Company would experience if a counterparty to a financial instrument failed to meet\nL5141@P121: its obligations in accordance with the terms and conditions of its contracts with the Company.\nL5142@P121: Counterparty credit risk is managed through established credit management techniques, including conducting financial and other assessments\nL5143@P121: to establish and monitor a counterparty’s creditworthiness, setting exposure limits, monitoring exposures against these limits, using master\nL5144@P121: netting arrangements and obtaining financial assurances where warranted. In general, financial assurances include guarantees, letters of credit\nL5145@P121: and cash. The Company monitors and manages its concentration of counterparty credit risk on an ongoing basis. The Company believes these\nL5146@P121: measures minimize its counterparty credit risk but there is no certainty that they will protect it against all material losses.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNovartis Group Annual Report 2010 (https://www.novartis.com/sites/novartiscom/files/novartis-annual-report-2010-en.pdf)\nciteturn182view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 274; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.novartis.com/sites/novartiscom/files/novartis-annual-report-2010-en.pdf\",\"pattern\":\"Novartis Corporation\"}); Total lines: 15704\nL13160@P230: and Reclast (zoledronic acid 5mg) injection patent. Teva has\nL13161@P230: dropped the challenge against the Novartis patent and will not\nL13162@P230: launch zoledronic acid in the US until after the Zometa and Reclast\nL13163@P230: patent expires in March 2013. The case was dismissed in July 2010\nL13164@P230-231: and is therefore concluded.230 NOVARTIS GROUP ANNUAL REPORT 2010\nL13165@P231: 20. PROVISIONS AND OTHER NON-CURRENT LIABILITIES (CONTINUED)\nL13166@P231: Gender discrimination litigation\nL13167@P231: In November 2004, certain female pharmaceutical sales represen\u0002tatives brought a class action lawsuit in the SDNY against NPC,\nL13168@P231: Novartis Corporation and a Novartis executive alleging claims of\nL13169@P231: gender discrimination. Novartis Corporation and the Novartis exec\u0002utive were subsequently dismissed from the lawsuit. The trial\nL13170@P231: against NPC began in April 2010. On May 17 and 19, 2010, the jury\nL13171@P231: rendered a liability verdict and awarded USD 3.4 million in individ\u0002ual compensatory damages to the class members testifying at trial\nL13172@P231: and USD 250 million in punitive damages. On July 14, 2010, the\nL13173@P231: SDNY preliminarily approved a class action settlement agreement\nL13174@P231: between NPC and the plaintiffs to end the ongoing proceedings. On\nL13175@P231: September 8, 2010, notice of the settlement was sent to all class\nL13176@P231: members. The fairness hearing in the SDNY took place on Novem\u0002ber 19, 2010, and on November 30, 2010, the SDNY issued an\nL13177@P231: order granting final approval of the settlement, dismissing the class\nL13178@P231: action with prejudice and therefore concluding this case.\nL13179@P231: According to the class action settlement agreement NPC will\nL13180@P231: make monetary payments to eligible class members for backpay\nL13181@P231: and compensatory damages in the amount of up to USD 152.5 mil\u0002lion and will fund, over three years, improvements to policies and\nL13182@P231: programs valued at an estimated USD 22.5 million. As part of the\nL13183@P231: measures, NPC will enhance many of its ongoing commitments to\nL13184@P231: all employees and will add additional programs and initiatives to\nL13185@P231: further strengthen its commitment to a diverse and inclusive envi\u0002ronment. NPC will for example revise its sexual harassment policy\nL13186@P231: and training, strengthen its complaint process to ensure employees\nL13187@P231: can safely raise concerns and that those concerns will be addressed\nL14262@P248: Novartis Vaccines and Diagnostics S.r.l., Siena EUR 41.5 m 100 ◆▼▲\nL14263@P248: Sandoz S.p.A., Origgio EUR 390 000 100 ◆\nL14264@P248: Sandoz Industri